    "summary": "Rebuilt and validated the single-span simply supported precast small-box girder bridge (25 m, 5 girders, deck width 16 m, Highway Class II, 4 lanes).\n\nRoot cause of the failure: the instance girder section had top slab thickness t_top = 0.2 m, contradicting the description (\"top slab 0.18 m thick\"; the geometry integrates to A_single = 1.0863 m² / I = 0.25476 m⁴ instead of the accepted 1.06052 m² / 0.25166 m⁴). This broke the L2-1 consistency check between the accepted section-property decisions and the model's recomputed geometry. Fixing t_top to 0.18 m (with the rest of the small-box geometry: h=1.4, b_top=2.4, t_tip=0.18, haunch 0.201/0.062, b_bottom=1.0, t_bottom=0.18, t_web=0.2, web_slope=3.5, chamfers 0.15×0.07) restored consistency; the girder section-property decisions were re-proposed and re-accepted unchanged.\n\nKey results after regeneration:\n- f1 = 4.272 Hz (first vertical bending), μ = 0.241 by JTG D60-2015 4.3.2.\n- girder.A = 5.3026 m², girder.I = 1.2583 m⁴, w = 137.87 kN/m, m = 14.054 t/m.\n- Self-weight support reactions: 1723.35 kN per support line (sum 3446.69 kN = total girder self-weight), midspan moment 10 770.9 kN·m.\n- Lane-load max positive moment: 6878.4 kN·m at midspan.\n- Supports: two abutments, each with 10 plain laminated elastomeric bearings (2 per girder end; 300×350 mm, 4×8 mm rubber layers), Kv = 14.16×10⁶ kN/m, Kh = 32 812 kN/m per line.\n\nThe model now passes all L1 topology, L2 code and L3 mechanics checks."
